-    <main class="page-container"><a href="/HermeticDB/scholars.html" class="back-link">← Return to Faculty</a><h1 class="title-large">Roger Bacon</h1><div class="card-meta" style="margin-bottom:3rem">Scholarly Authority</div><div class="prose-content"><p>Roger Bacon (c. 1214–1292), known as the <i>Doctor Mirabilis</i>, was an English Franciscan friar and polymath. He is often cited as an early advocate of the experimental method (<i>scientia experimentalis</i>), arguing that reasoning alone could never provide certainty without the confirmation of experience.</p>
-        <h2>Historical Context</h2>
-        <p>Bacon’s <i>Opus Maius</i>, sent to Pope Clement IV, envisioned a total reform of the sciences, including alchemy, which he divided into "speculative" and "operative" branches. He believed that alchemy could not only transform metals but also prolong human life (the Elixir of Life). His cryptic writings and interest in "miracles of art and nature" led to his later reputation as a sorcerer and the legendary inventor of gunpowder.</p>
-        <h2>Scholarly Significance</h2>
-        <p>Contemporary analysis by William Newman has highlighted Bacon's sophisticated understanding of the "art-nature" relationship. He saw alchemy as the highest form of natural philosophy, because it did not merely observe nature but perfected it through human intervention, a core Hermetic theme.</p></div></main>
+    <main class="page-container"><a href="/HermeticDB/scholars.html" class="back-link">← Return to Faculty</a><h1 class="title-large">Roger Bacon</h1><div class="card-meta" style="margin-bottom:3rem">Scholarly Authority</div><div class="prose-content"><p>Roger Bacon (c. 1214/1220–1292) was an English Franciscan friar and philosopher, active at Oxford and Paris. He is celebrated as a pioneer of empirical science and the systematic use of mathematics in natural philosophy.</p>
+        <h2>Hermes as Scientific Authority</h2>
+        <p>Bacon repeatedly cited Hermes Trismegistus as a positive authority in his major works, including the <i>Opus Majus</i>. For Bacon, Hermes represented the ancient tradition of <i>scientia experimentalis</i> — knowledge derived from direct experience — making him a proto-scientist rather than a mystic. Bacon drew on the Hermetic astrology and optical theory preserved in Arabic sources (particularly al-Kindi's <i>De Radiis</i>) to develop his theory of the 'multiplication of species' (the propagation of natural forces through the medium of light). His engagement illustrates how Hermetic cosmological principles (correspondence, <i>Sympatheia</i>, number) entered the foundations of early medieval natural science.</p></div></main>
